  "read": {
    "headline": "The banks fell, oil fell for a sixth day, and the two-year auction cost 58bp more than last month's.",
    "standfirst": "Tuesday closed flat on the surface — the S&P 500 lost 0.06 points to 7,764.64, the Nasdaq rose 0.45% to a second straight record at 27,244.28, the Dow fell 0.36% — with financials down 1.97% as the day's loser. Underneath, Treasury sold $69 billion of two-years at 4.787%, the highest stop since May 2024, and Brent settled at $99.25 for its first close under $100 since 9 September, then kept falling overnight. WTI's front month rolled to November, so its level below reads about $5 lower than Tuesday's settle without a $5 move.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The sector that fell on Tuesday was the banks, not energy.",
        "text": "Financials lost 1.97%, the widest fall on the board, with the large banks leading; energy was second at 1.09%. Materials rose 1.65% and staples 0.99%. Financials are now up 0.05% year to date — the only sector with a year of nothing — against technology's 36.33% and energy's 38.18%. A two-year at 4.79% and a ten-year at 4.96% is a flat curve for a lender, and the tape has started to price it as one."
      },
      {
        "title": "WTI did not fall $5 overnight. Its front month changed.",
        "text": "The October contract settled at $94.59 on Tuesday; the November contract now trades at $89.31, and that is the row above. The gap between the two is the backwardation — the premium the market pays for a barrel now over a barrel in six weeks — and it is why the five-day column reads −10.96% for WTI against −5.05% for Brent. The real move is six straight sessions down and a first Brent close under $100 since 9 September. Natural gas has gone the other way: +8.86% over five days and +13.95% over the month."
      },
      {
        "title": "The two-year in the source table reads 4.71%. The cash close was 4.75%.",
        "text": "The curve above shows 2s10s at 25bp, up from 20 yesterday — but that widening is the source table's two-year print, not the market's. Reported closes were 4.749% on the two-year and 4.966% on the ten, which is 2s10s at 22bp and a curve that barely moved. The number that did move was the auction: $69 billion of two-years stopped at 4.787%, the highest since May 2024 and 58bp above August's, on a bid-to-cover of 2.63. Demand was there; the price was higher."
      },
      {
        "title": "Korea is running away from Taiwan, and from everything else.",
        "text": "The Korea ETF rose 1.83% on Tuesday while Taiwan fell 0.46%; over five days Korea is up 6.24% and over a month 10.93%. On the twelve-month column it is 136.49%, against Taiwan's 79.05% and the United States' 16.61%. About a fifth of the month's move is the won, which has gained 2.15% on the dollar. Japan shows 0.00% over five days because Tokyo was shut Tuesday and is shut again today for Silver Week; the Nikkei's 65,018.95 is Monday's close."
      },
      {
        "title": "Coffee is the worst month on the board and nobody is talking about it.",
        "text": "Coffee is down 20.30% over a month and 7.55% over five days, more than any energy contract, and is now off 21.92% year to date. Lean hogs bounced 1.72% on Tuesday after Monday's 10.63% fall and are still down 9.09% on the week. Cocoa, the other soft that has been moving, is up 66.99% over six months and down 22.74% over twelve — the same contract, two opposite stories, depending on where you start the clock."
      },
      {
        "title": "The dollar is up because the Fed's non-voters keep talking.",
        "text": "The euro is down 2.04% on the dollar over a month and sterling 2.33%; the dollar closed Tuesday at its highest against the Canadian dollar since late July, at 1.41. Barkin, Collins, Goolsbee and Musalem have all leaned toward more tightening in the past two sessions, none of them votes this year, and the Chair has said nothing. Futures put 28 October at about 55% and December near 89%. A dollar bid on speeches is a dollar bid that a speech can unwind."
      }
    ]
  },
